          "Computer networks can be classified according to their transmission medium, geographical size, network organisation type and physical layout.",
          "10.2.1 Wired and Wireless Networks Networks can be classified as wired or wireless based on transmission medium.",
          "10.2.1.1 Wired Networks A wired network is a network of devices connected by physical cables. Data transfer is typically faster and more secure in a wired network. However, as the number of devices and the distance between devices increase, the cost of setting up the network increases as well.",
          "Ethernet is the most widely used wired network and it is standard in home networks. Figure 10.1 shows the connector of an Ethernet cable that is used to connect devices in a wired network. An Ethernet cable is typically made using twisted pairs of copper wires. The twisting helps to reduce interference, improving the cable’s ability to transmit data.",
          "DID YOU KKNNOOWW??",
          "Besides Ethernet, wired networks may also use fibre optic cables that transmit data using light. Fiber optics are ideal for long-distance communication because they provide faster transmission speeds and minimal susceptibility to interference, unlike copper wires typically used in Ethernet cables.",
          "Despite these advantages, the higher cost associated with fibre optic cables and related equipment often makes them a less common choice for home networking.",
          "Figure 10.2 A bundle of optical fibres Figure 10.3 A fibre optic cable with protective sheathing and connectors 10.2.1.2 Wireless Network A wireless network is a network of devices in which signals are transmitted without the use of physical cables. The transmission is in the form of electromagnetic waves, such as radio waves and microwaves.",
          "Users can be connected to a wireless network if they are within range of the network coverage.",
