          "Before transmission, the checksum for the data is first calculated. For instance, a simple checksum that is one byte in length may be calculated by treating all the bytes in the data as numbers between 0 and 255 (inclusive), summing them up and calculating the result modulus 256. There are also many other possible checksum algorithms.",
          "The data and the checksum are then sent together. At the destination, the checksum is recalculated and compared to the sent checksum value. If the checksum value of the received data matches the sent checksum value, the data was transmitted correctly. If they differ, an error has occurred. The recipient can then request for the data to be sent again.",
          "Figure 10.10 demonstrates how the checksum for the data “Hello, World!” may be calculated using the checksum algorithm described previously.",
