          "10.3.4 Echo Checks In an echo check, when data is received, the receiver sends a copy of what was received back to the sender so the sender can compare it with what was originally sent. If the two sets of data are different, the sender sends the data again.",
          "Table 10.9 shows the advantages and disadvantages of using echo checks for error detection.",
          "Advantages Disadvantages • Detects more kinds of errors compared to • Echo checks roughly double the amount parity bits and checksums of traffic and time needed to complete each transmission • Without further checks, the sender cannot tell if the error occurred during the initial transmission (which requires resending) or when the echo was transmitted (which does not require resending) • By itself, an echo check does not address a situation where data is lost and the receiver receives no data at all.",
          "KEY TERMS Echo check An error detection method where the receiver sends a copy of what was received back to the sender 10.3.5 Automatic Repeat Requests Besides data corruption, packets can also be lost during transmission. To address both possibilities that packets may become lost or corrupted, we can use Automatic Repeat Requests (ARQ). When data is received using ARQ, the receiver sends a positive acknowledgement (ACK) back to the sender if no errors are detected or a negative acknowledgement (NACK) if any errors are detected. On the other end, the sender waits for an acknowledgement (either positive or negative) from the receiver to arrive within a predetermined amount of time called a timeout. There are then three possible scenarios:",
