          "• Bandwidth: The network needs to support many users (about 120) with the possibility of business expansion in the future. Servers are designed to use bandwidth more efficiently and offer more reliable connections. On the other hand, in a P2P network the bandwidth of the network could decrease if a single device must handle many large requests or if requests must rely on a device with an unreliable connection.",
          "• Security: As the accountancy consulting business requires the using and handling of financial data, the nature of the business calls for high confidentiality. A client-server network is best for implementing a secure system where users are required to authenticate to a network, while a P2P network offers little to no centralised control over security.",
          "• Storage: The business has a large data store dating back to 10 years ago. A client-server network enables data to be stored centrally and is best for supporting businesses requiring large data storage as storage servers can be added and maintained centrally. In a P2P network, data is decentralised and stored on clients instead of on a server. This may limit the amount of data that can be stored as a typical user’s device would have smaller storage space compared to a dedicated server.",
