          "Many ML algorithms require performing repetitive calculations on a large amount of data. For instance, the NN method requires calculating the distance from the new data point to every data point in the training data. Libraries such as scikit-learn can speed up this process by taking advantage of hardware such as GPUs that are specifically designed to accelerate such operations.",
          "14.1.5 Risks and Unintended Consequences While AI can automate many complex tasks traditionally performed by humans, doing so comes with inherent risks that need careful management to avoid unintended consequences.",
          "14.1.5.1 Overfitting and Underfitting In ML, overfitting occurs when a model learns not only the underlying patterns in the training data but also the random noise that may be present in the training data. This noise can arise from various sources, such as measurement errors or exceptional outliers that are significantly different from the majority. When a model becomes overly specialised to the training data, it starts to memorise the noise rather than learning generalisable patterns. As a result, overfitting typically results in high accuracy on training data but poor generalisation when applying the model to new data (i.e., inference).",
          "Overfitting may occur if the amount of training data is too small (relative to the model’s complexity) or unbalanced, meaning that the training data does not adequately represent the full range of possible data points necessary for effective learning. To mitigate this, in ML it is important to assemble a sufficiently large and diverse set of data points for training.",
          "Conversely, underfitting occurs when a model is too simple to learn the underlying patterns of the training data effectively. As an example, for the task of classifying songs by genre, a one-dimensional NN model that only considers song length (and ignores song tempo) would be too simple to differentiate between the three genres effectively. To avoid underfitting, it is important to choose a sufficiently complex model that can capture the patterns present in the data.",
