          "There have been numerous instances where bias in AI systems led to unintended consequences. For example, in the US, an AI model used for predicting healthcare needs was found to have underestimated the care required by African American patients. This was because the model predicted healthcare needs based on healthcare costs, indirectly disadvantaging African American patients who historically incurred lower healthcare costs not due to lower needs, but because of reduced access to more expensive treatments.",
          "14.1.5.3 Privacy and Surveillance ML requires large amounts of data to work well. Sometimes, this data includes personal information collected or used without the clear permission of the individuals involved. With the growing need for AI systems that are tailored to individuals and perform complex tasks, many companies and governments might be tempted to collect, buy, or sell personal data for training purposes. For example, a social media platform could sell its database of user posts and comments to another company to train their AI chatbot.",
          "Similarly, a government might use images from public security cameras and government records to enhance a facial recognition system, often without fully informing the people in those images.",
          "Furthermore, AI technologies like facial recognition make it easy to identify and track individuals through video surveillance. People who think they are browsing the internet anonymously might also be tracked by AI systems. These systems can create an online “fingerprint” from browsing habits, device details, and even typing patterns. Such tracking capabilities can lead to a significant loss of privacy, as both companies and governments gain the ability to monitor individuals more closely and accurately.",
          "To reduce these privacy and surveillance risks, privacy laws play an important role by setting the rules for how organisations can collect personal data and establish penalties for breaking these rules. For instance, in Singapore the PDPA requires organisations to seek consent, disclose the purpose of collecting personal data (e.g., for training AI), and retain personal data only as long as needed to fulfil its specified purpose.",
          "Users should also be educated to understand the privacy policy of services before using them and to use defences such as encryption to protect the confidentiality of personal data.",
          "For more information on threats to privacy and possible defences, see Chapter 11.",
          "14.1.5.4 Copyright Ownership and Infringement AI’s ability to generate content raises complex issues with regards to intellectual property rights and norms.",
          "Some of these issues are:",
          "Ownership of AI-Generated Content In many countries (including Singapore), human authorship is required for copyright protection.",
          "Hence, in the case of AI-generated content where human involvement may be limited, it is unclear who the copyright owner should be. For example, in 2022, the US Copyright Office denied copyright protection for AI-generated artwork in a graphic novel as it found that the individual images lacked sufficient human creativity. However, the office found that the graphic novel’s text and its arrangement of text and images still qualified for copyright protection as these elements had clear human authorship and demonstrated sufficient creativity.",
          "Training on Copyrighted Material Generative AI systems require training on large amounts of data, which may include copyrighted materials that have been collected or used without the explicit permission of the copyright owners.",
          "However, there is an argument that this may be considered acceptable “fair use” of copyrighted materials for research. As of 2024, many providers of generative AI systems are facing lawsuits from various copyright owners on the unauthorised use of their content for training AI, and the outcome of many of these lawsuits is still uncertain.",
          "Generation of Infringing Content Generative AI systems may generate content that closely resembles copyrighted materials, such as producing artworks that mimic an artist’s style, even when not prompted to do so explicitly. A generative AI model that is overfitted may even memorise the training data and output an exact copy of any copyrighted material that it was trained on. Depending on the similarity between the generated content and the copyrighted material, this may constitute plagiarism and/or copyright infringement. Therefore, it is crucial to acknowledge the use of generative AI as well as to verify and cite the sources used by the AI to generate the content.",
          "To address these issues, new legal frameworks and licensing agreements may be needed between generative AI providers and copyright owners to ensure that creators are compensated fairly while encouraging innovation and the development of new AI technologies.",
          "For more information on intellectual property, see Chapter 12.",
          "14.1.5.5 Spread of Misinformation Generative AI systems can produce audio content, images and videos that are highly realistic but are either wholly or partially fake. They can also create convincing deepfakes that depict people doing things they never actually did. When used unethically, such fake AI-generated content can be used to maliciously manipulate public opinion, damage reputations and/or boost engagement on social media.",
