          "Eliminating such safety risks may be difficult as AI systems built on ML do not have explicit rules or instructions that can be checked thoroughly, and their behaviour may be probabilistic such that their output varies each time with the same input.",
          "These challenges motivate the need to prioritise transparency and risk management in the use of AI. For applications where the consequences of risk are severe, having an AI system operate completely without human supervision may be inappropriate. For other applications where some risk is acceptable, there should be clear guidelines for accountability if things go wrong.",
          "DID YOU KKNNOOWW??",
          "One of the challenges in ensuring the safety of ML systems stems from their reliance on “neural networks”, which are complex and not easily interpretable. Neural networks, inspired by the human brain, consist of layers of artificial “neurons”. These neurons process inputs based on their parameters, or “weights,” and pass their outputs to subsequent neurons. When a neural network incorporates multiple layers, this training process is known as “deep learning.” Training begins with neurons assigned random weights, which are systematically adjusted to minimise output errors using training data. This makes neural networks and deep learning extremely effective for a variety of AI applications, including image classification, voice recognition, and content generation. However, because the training process depends entirely on the data and lacks human oversight, understanding the functions of the numerous neurons and the significance of their weights can be challenging.",
          "Simple Neural Network Deep Learning Neural Network Input Layer Hidden Layer Output Layer 14.1.5.7 Changes to the Job Market AI’s ability to perform many complex tasks that are traditionally performed by humans may lead to significant changes in the job market as AI is used to automate tasks, making certain jobs obsolete. While the impact will be greatest for jobs that involve repetitive and routine tasks, the advent of generative AI may also affect analytical and creative jobs that were previously thought to be immune to automation.",
          "To mitigate these effects, it is important to develop and promote continuous education and training programmes to help workers acquire new skills that will be in demand as the job market evolves.",
          "These would include skills that cannot be easily performed by AI such as critical thinking, empathy, and interpersonal communication. It will also be important to encourage the development of new industries and roles that can absorb the workforce displaced by AI. For instance, while AI may automate certain tasks, new jobs in AI maintenance, supervision, and improvement may arise.",
