          "14.2 Other Emerging Technologies LEARNING OUTCOMES 5.4.1 Show an awareness of emerging technologies.",
          "14.2.1 Blockchain A blockchain is a list of records that exists on multiple computers in a network such that each time a record is added, it cannot be changed or removed. It is often used to securely track financial transactions without relying on a central authority (e.g., a bank).",
          "Records on a blockchain are grouped into KEY TERMS “blocks” and linked together in chronological order. Each block contains a calculated hash Blockchain that is mathematically related like a checksum A list of records that exists on multiple computers in to: (1) the block’s content, (2) a timestamp and a network such that each time a record is added, it (3) the previous block’s hash. Any alteration of cannot be changed or removed or tampering with these elements would break this mathematical relationship and hence be Hash detected. In this way, every block is securely A calculated value that is mathematically related to connected to the one before it, forming a some data such that any altering of the data would continuous chain such that one can quickly break this mathematical relationship and hence be detect attempts to tamper with any record detected along the chain.",
          "By itself, this chain of hashes is not enough to ensure that a record cannot be changed or removed after it is added. For instance, an attacker might attempt to alter a block and then recalculate all subsequent hashes to cover up the tampering.",
          "To defend against such attacks, a blockchain is always distributed across multiple computers so that any changes to the blockchain must be verified and accepted by these computers via a set of rules to achieve consensus; otherwise, the change is rejected. For instance, one type of consensus requires “proof of work” and involves turning the calculation of a block’s hash into a mathematical puzzle that requires significant computing power to solve. This mechanism makes it prohibitively difficult and expensive to successfully tamper with the blockchain as it would require the attacker to both recalculate the hashes of all subsequent blocks and control the majority of computers on the blockchain’s network.",
          "The main properties of a blockchain are:",
          "Decentralisation Transparency Records are stored and validated by a All records on the blockchain are visible network of multiple computers without a to anyone who has access. In a public central authority. blockchain, all records are publicly viewable.",
          "Security Immutability Any addition of blocks must be verified Once a block is added to a blockchain, and accepted by computers in the it cannot be changed or deleted. This network via a set of rules to achieve immutability is partially ensured by hash consensus. Each record is also digitally functions, where each block references signed to verify its origin and prevent the hash of the preceding block, linking forgery. them securely and creating a continuous chain that can quickly detect any tampering.",
          "As of 2024, the most well-known application of blockchains is in cryptocurrencies. A cryptocurrency is a virtual currency that is secured using cryptography and operates without relying on a central authority. For instance, cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin typically have transactions that are digitally signed to prevent forgery and are tracked using blockchains. Transactions involving cryptocurrencies can also be anonymous as only digital identifiers are needed, not real-world identities that a central authority such as a bank might require. While cryptocurrencies have benefits in terms of decentralisation, transparency and privacy, their value can be highly unstable and potential investors should be aware of their significant risk.",
          "Besides cryptocurrency transactions, blockchains have KEY TERMS also been used for the secure tracking of goods, health records, educational certificates, election votes, and so on. Cryptocurrency As an emerging technology, it is expected that blockchain A virtual currency that is secured using technology will continue to expand into various other cryptography and operates without industries in the future. relying on a central authority 14.2.2 Virtual Reality and Augmented Reality Virtual Reality (VR) is a technology that immerses users in a completely computer-generated artificial environment. In contrast, Augmented Reality (AR) enhances the user’s real-world environment with digital elements. Although various forms of VR and AR have been in development since the 1990s, it was not until the late 2000s, with the proliferation of smartphones, that these technologies saw significant advancements.",
          "The large-scale demand and production of smartphones resulted in substantial improvements and cost reductions to high-resolution displays and advanced sensors. These developments have enabled the creation of more sophisticated and accessible VR and AR systems.",
          "A typical VR system has a few essential components:",
          "1. Displays: A VR user typically needs to wear a Head-Mounted Device (HMD) or headset with two displays, one for each eye. This allows the system to display a slightly different perspective for each eye, simulating 3D depth.",
          "2. Head Tracking System: A typical HMD would usually have various sensors (e.g., accelerometers, gyroscopes and magnetometers) that allow basic tracking of the user’s head movement.",
          "For greater accuracy, some VR systems incorporate cameras on the HMD itself.",
          "These cameras use sophisticated algorithms to identify and track distinct features within the user’s environment. Figure 14.7 An example of a Head-Mounted Device or headset This method, known as “inside- out” tracking, calculates the HMD’s position and orientation in real-time based on the observed environmental features.",
          "Alternatively, other VR systems rely on “outside-in” tracking, which involves external cameras or sensors positioned within the environment. These sensors monitor specific markers on the HMD, providing precise tracking data. While outside-in tracking generally offers greater accuracy, it also requires a more complex setup, which can be less convenient for the user.",
          "3. Additional Input Devices: VR systems typically support a variety of input devices such as handheld controllers, gloves and treadmills for interacting with or moving in the virtual environment. Some systems support bodysuits or attachable trackers for tracking the user’s pose and full-body movements.",
          "Cameras on the HMD may also track the user’s eye movements and facial expressions.",
          "4. Processor: To process the tracking data and generate images to display on the HMD, a computer processor is typically needed. The processor can be located on the HMD itself or on an external device.",
          "As of 2024, while VR systems are largely similar, AR KEY TERMS systems can vary greatly in terms of the design and level of immersion. For instance, AR systems can Augmented reality (AR) range from simple applications on a smartphone A technology that enhances the user’s real-world which overlay digital information onto the real environment with digital elements world through the phone’s camera and screen, to more complex systems like AR glasses or HMDs that Head-mounted device (HMD) or headset project images directly to the user’s eyes. The level A display device that is worn on the head, typically of immersion can also vary from minimal, such as part of a VR or AR system as adding simple data overlays, to more complex interactions where digital objects are integrated Virtual reality (VR) into the real world in a way that they seem to A technology that immerses users in a completely interact physically with real-world elements. computer-generated artificial environment AR Glasses VR Headset Figure 14.8 Examples of AR glasses and a VR headset Both VR and AR have a broad range of applications across various fields:",
          "1. Education and Training: VR and AR can simulate complex environments for training and education purposes. Medical students can practice surgeries, and mechanics can learn engine assembly without suffering real-world consequences.",
          "2. Gaming and Entertainment: VR games provide immersive gaming experiences, while AR games can blend digital objects with the real world.",
          "3. Healthcare: VR is used for therapy and pain management, allowing patients to experience peaceful environments to manage pain or anxiety. AR helps surgeons visualize bones and organs during surgery to avoid making errors.",
          "4. Retail: AR apps allow customers to visualise products in their own home before buying, enhancing customer satisfaction and reducing return rates.",
          "5. Real Estate and Architecture: VR tours can show prospective buyers around a property from anywhere in the world, while architects use AR to overlay proposed designs onto existing spaces.",
          "14.2.3 Quantum Computing Quantum mechanics is the study of behaviour at the subatomic (i.e., smaller than atomic) scale. At this very small scale, subatomic particles such as electrons behave in ways that can seem strange or counterintuitive. Some strange behaviours that have been observed are:",
          "• Superposition of states: Particles like electrons can exist in multiple states (e.g., possess different amounts of energy) at the same time. However, when the particle is measured or observed, it will settle into one of those states with certain probabilities that can be predicted using quantum mechanics.",
          "• Entanglement: If two quantum particles are produced in a way that the final state of one particle is linked to the state of the other (e.g., by “exciting” an atom or ion so that it emits a pair of light particles known as photons), then these two particles are said to be “entangled.” A surprising and key aspect of entanglement is that if we measure the state of one particle, the state of the other particle is instantly determined, no matter how far apart the two particles are.",
          "• Wave-particle duality: Quantum particles such as electrons and photons can act as both particles and waves. For instance, when a stream of electrons travels through two slits onto a screen, each electron would be detected like a particle when it hits the screen, but over time the pattern formed by the electrons would correspond to an interference pattern for two waves with multiple bands.",
          "Double-slit Screen Electrons Electron Beam Gun Interference pattern Q m u e a c n ha tu n m ics t c o o p m e p rf u o t r i m ng c om is pu th ta e t io u n s s e . T o h f e b q a u s a ic n t u u n m it KEY TERMS of information in quantum computing is a quantum bit or qubit. Unlike a “classical” bit that is always either 0 or 1, a qubit can be a superposition of both Quantum computing states. This allows a qubit to carry more information The use of quantum mechanics to perform than a classical bit and enables quantum computers computations to evaluate multiple possibilities at once.",
          "Quantum mechanics The study of behaviour at the subatomic scale Qubits can also be entangled, meaning the state of one qubit can instantly influence another, Qubit regardless of the distance between them. This A quantum bit that can be a superposition of entanglement allows qubits to represent more 0 and 1 complex relationships than classical bits can, thereby enabling quantum computers to efficiently perform operations that involve multiple qubits.",
          "In classical computers, bits are manipulated using logic gates, as introduced in Chapter 3. Quantum computers, however, operate using quantum logic gates that work on qubits. These gates exploit the wave nature of quantum particles through interference, allowing specialised algorithms to manage the states of multiple qubits and enhance the likelihood of obtaining the correct outcomes.",
          "These advanced capabilities allow quantum computers to solve certain types of problems such as factoring large numbers and searching databases much more quickly than classical computers. The ability to factor large numbers quickly is particularly significant as many current encryption methods rely on the difficulty of factoring large numbers for their security.",
          "As of 2024, quantum computers are primarily in the experimental stage, with limited qubit numbers and susceptibility to errors. However, as this technology progresses, improvements in both performance and accessibility are anticipated. To prepare for this future, there has been a significant push to develop and adopt new quantum-resistant encryption techniques that can safeguard data in the era of quantum computing.",
